  "reveal": {
    "ledger_voiced": "From my book. The sitting ran as I ran it: the touch: a kid glove, stuffed with wool and kept on the ice; the table's tilt: the wedge of my boot beneath the claw-foot; the whispered voice: my own voice, thrown low; grief hears what it is owed; the spirit trumpet: black silk thread, and the reach of a practised arm; the spirit writing: the slates changed in the dark; chalk-crumb on my cuff; the cold draught: the window cord, loosed an inch before the lamps went out; the guttering of the lamp: a wick trimmed short at six o'clock; it wants ten minutes.\n\nAs to the evening's business: the account Nan Pringle swore to was an invention, for those hands were out of the dark and about their own work. Aziz was past any sitting's reach before the lamps were lit.\n\nThe dead say what it profits me they say; on this occasion they profited Nan Pringle more.",
